{"name":"Darling Clementine","hex":"#d29f7a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"darling","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Often used as an affectionate term of address: a person who is very dear to one.","A person who is kind, sweet, etc., and thus lovable; a pet, a sweetheart; also, an animal or thing which is cute and lovable."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Very dear; beloved, cherished, favourite.","Very cute or lovable; adorable, charming, sweet."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/darling"},{"term":"clementine","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A type of small, sweet-tasting orange, the result of a hybrid cross between the willowleaf variety of mandarin orange (Citrus × aurantium f. deliciosa) and a sweet orange (Citrus sinensis). Scientific name: Citrus × clementina."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/clementine"}],"entries":[{"title":"Clementine","blocks":[{"text":"A clementine is a tangor, a citrus fruit hybrid between a willowleaf mandarin orange and a sweet orange, named in honor of Clément Rodier, a French missionary who first discovered and propagated the cultivar in Algeria. The exterior is a deep orange colour with a smooth, glossy appearance. Clementines can be separated into 7 to 14 segments. Similar to tangerines, they tend to be easy to peel. They are typically juicy and sweet, with less acid than oranges. Their oils, like other citrus fruits, contain mostly limonene as well as myrcene, linalool, α-pinene and many complex aromatics."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Clementines_whole%2C_peeled%2C_half_and_sectioned.jpg/330px-Clementines_whole%2C_peeled%2C_half_and_sectioned.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clementine"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Clementine_del_golfo_di_Taranto_IGP.jpg/500px-Clementine_d